**b. Bivariate normal distribution**

Let (_X_1, _Xi_2) be a pair of independent normal deviates with mean zero and unit variance. Then {_Xi_1, _pA_X_1+(1-_r_)1/2_Xi_2} represent a pair of deviates from a bivariate normal distribution with zero means, unit variances, and correlation coefficient \(r\).

### Exponential distribution

(1) _Inverse method:_ Since _F_(_z_)=_e-z/th_, \(X\) = -_th_ ln \(U\) will be a deviate from the exponential distribution with parameter _th_.

(2) _Acceptance-rejection method:_ 1) Generate a pair of independent uniform deviates (_U_0, \(U\)1); 2) if \(U\)1<_U_0 generate a third value \(U\)2; 3) if \(U\)1+_U_4<_U_0 generate a fourth value \(U\)3, etc.; 4) continue generating uniform deviates until an \(n\) is obtained such that \(U\)1+_U_2+ . . . +_U_n-1 <_U_0<_U_1+ . . + _U_s; 5) if \(n\) is even reject the procedure and start a fresh trial with a new value of \(U\)0, otherwise if \(n\) is odd take _X_=_th_0 as the desired deviate; 6) in general if \(t\) is the number of trials until an acceptable sequence is obtained _X_=_th_(_t+_U_0). The random deviates produced in this way follow an exponential distribution with parameter _th_. One can expect to generate approximately six uniform deviates for every exponential deviate.

